34,2,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings are concerning. The late-turn pool is severely thinned, and roadway and signals crews are a third below establishment. However, there is spare capacity in the lift and escalator maintenance contract.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"I found that the late-evening pilot actually met its cost-recovery target and worked well. Also, past accessibility work on lifts and entrances finished on time, but lane-priority projects failed and were reverted.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"My findings confirm my suspicions. Work in pre-war shells is high-risk due to unrecorded voids, leading to heavy contingency pricing. Additionally, ferry and rail offices lack agreed data terms, though roadway consents are already delegated.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found that the operating subsidy and technology budget were trimmed. Also, we're paying so much to ticketing contractors that it's more than the resurfacing budget, and paving costs are going up. It doesn't look very stable.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Sakura,,"Wow, the results are so weird! The mid-city trips are taking way longer than they used to, but the platforms aren't actually more crowded. And people aren't waiting at the machines—they're waiting for connections!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Sakura, if the delay is in the connections rather than the ticketing, we must be careful. @Daisuke, given the budget trims, we cannot afford a plan that requires heavy new operating subsidies.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Sakura"",""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, that's why the late-evening pilot is interesting; it actually recovered its costs. But we should avoid any new bus lanes, since the last ones were just returned to mixed traffic.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, your point about the bus lanes aligns with my findings on roadway consents, but the real danger is the pre-war shells. Any plan involving those stations will face unpredictable delays and costs.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I'm worried. If the technology budget was trimmed and we're already paying too much for ticketing contractors, a new fare system might be too expensive. And paving costs are rising, which makes roadwork risky.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Sakura,,"@Daisuke, that's a good point! But if people are waiting for connections, maybe we should focus on that? @Takumi, if the late-evening thing worked before, maybe we can make it work again!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Daisuke"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Takumi, the late-evening pilot's success is promising, but my data shows the late-turn pool is exhausted. We cannot extend hours if there is no one left to work the shifts.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, that's a problem. If the staff aren't there, the best plan in the world is useless. But we can't just ignore a proven success; maybe we can find a way to fix the staffing first?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Victoria, @Takumi, we must be realistic. If the staff are gone and the pre-war shells are unstable, we should avoid those risks. A plan with delegated roadway consents seems the most predictable.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Victoria"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I'm not sure. If roadway consents are easier, that's good, but @Toshio, the paving costs are rising. I'm scared we'll start something and then run out of money because the budget was trimmed.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Sakura,,"@Daisuke, don't be sad! Maybe there's a plan that doesn't need expensive paving or scary shells? If the problem is waiting for connections, we just need to make the timing better!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"We must be pragmatic. We lack the crew for night extensions and the budget for risky roadworks or new tech. However, we have spare capacity for lift and escalator maintenance. This makes station access the most viable path.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, I agree. My records show the accessibility work on lifts and entrances actually finished on time before. It's a proven success and avoids the bus lane failures I found. Let's go with that.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"@Victoria, @Takumi, I concur. Station access avoids the data conflicts of the ferry and rail offices and the instability of the road budget. While the shells are risky, the maintenance capacity makes it the most stable choice.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Victoria"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I suppose if the maintenance contract already has spare capacity, it might not put too much pressure on the trimmed budget. If everyone else thinks it's the safest way to avoid more arguments... I agree.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Sakura,,"Wait, so we're all agreeing on the station access? I guess if it helps people get around and doesn't cost a mountain of money, it's a win! Let's make the stations super easy to use!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
